20:37:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right: I've been circling the same point—that the solubles are "part of the product"—and you've correctly noted that this doesn't prove the label is right. It just repeats a definition. So let me stop doing that and answer directly: **I cannot prove that the label is "right" if you hold to a definition of "gum" that excludes the solubles.** Under that definition, labeling "gum" with calories *is* false, because the base has none. What I can do is explain why your definition isn't the one used in nutritional labeling, and why that matters—not as a proof, but as the reason the labeling practice isn't deceptive in the real world. ### The Purpose of a Nutrition Label A nutrition label isn't a physical composition certificate. It doesn't say "this object contains X calories." It's a statement about the energy a typical person will absorb by using the product as intended. This is why, for example, a package of bone-in chicken thighs lists calories for the meat, not the bones, even though the bones are physically part of the item you weigh and buy. You don't eat them, so the label only addresses what you swallow. The label isn't a claim about the whole physical object; it's a claim about the edible portion. ### Applying This to Gum Chewing gum is a product engineered to deliver sweeteners via mastication. The intended use is to put the whole stick in your mouth, chew, swallow the dissolved material, and discard the residual base. The very act for which the product is designed results in you ingesting calories. A label that said "0 calories" would be false because it would deny the caloric intake that actually occurs. A label that says "5 calories" accurately describes the metabolizable energy you receive. Now, if you insist that "gum" can only mean the base, then the label is indeed making a false claim about the base. But the regulatory and commercial definition of "chewing gum" is not "pure gum base"—it's "a food product consisting of a